WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.780
The three rules of hate speech.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.440
Rule one: the First Amendment protects all ideas: loving,
hateful, or in between.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.860
In the United States hate speech is just a
political label

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.120
like un-American speech or rude speech.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.200
Some people use the phrase broadly, some more
narrowly

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:19.410
but there's no legal definition because there
is no hate speech exception to the First Amendment.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.540
As the Supreme Court held in 1974:

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.380
"Under the First Amendment there is no such
thing as a false idea.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.230
However pernicious an opinion may seem,

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.420
we depend for its correction not on the conscience
of judges and juries

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.620
but on the competition of other ideas."

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:32.960
Or in 2017:

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.910
"...the proudest boast of our free speech
jurisprudence

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.550
is that we protect the freedom to express
'the thought that we hate.'"

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.820
That's from Matal V Tam, in which the government
denied a trademark to an Asian-American band

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.520
because the band's name, The Slants, was seen
by some as a racial slur.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.270
The government wasn't even trying to ban the
name,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.700
it was just denying a generally available
benefit, trademark registration,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.280
to people who used the name.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.060
But even that, the Court concluded, was unconstitutional
viewpoint discrimination

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.120
and thus violated the First Amendment.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.820
Rule 2: Some speech is not protected by the
First Amendment

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.800
but that's true regardless of whether it's
bigoted or hateful.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.920
For instance, threats of violence are constitutionally
unprotected.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.160
That includes all threats, racist threats,
threats of police officers,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.360
business owners, the President, anyone.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.000
Likewise, intentionally inciting immediate
violence is sometimes punishable.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.780
Classic example, giving a speech to a mob
outside a building

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.200
urging them to burn it down.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.740
But again it doesn't matter if the speech
is outside a synagogue,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.680
a police station, or a recycling center.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.000
Personal insults said to someone's face might
also be punishable as so-called fighting words.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.920
Again though that's true regardless of whether
the insults stem from personal hostility,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.800
or group hatred related to race, religion
and the like.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.679
Indeed in 1992 the Supreme Court struck down
an ordinance

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.280
that specifically targeted bigoted fighting
words.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.860
Rule 3: Hate crime laws are constitutional
so long as they punish violence or vandalism,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.060
not speech.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.160
The classic example is Wisconsin v Mitchell,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.840
the 1993 case in which the Supreme Court unanimously
upheld hate crime laws.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.660
Todd Mitchell, a black man, urged some friends
to beat up a white boy because the boy was white.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.000
Wisconsin law made the beating into a more
serious crime

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.179
because the boy was targeted based on his race.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.460
The Court said this is fine because

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.340
"...a
physical assault is not by any stretch of the imagination

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.060
expressive conduct protected by the First
Amendment."

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.980
And while the law increased the punishment
because of the defendant's intent

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.880
the law often punishes more because of why
they did what they did.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.220
Killing someone for money will get you a harsher
punishment

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.200
than killing them out of momentary anger.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.780
Likewise, firing an employee because of his
race will get you a civil lawsuit,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.200
firing an employee for most other reasons
won't.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.130
None of this covers the mere expression of
hateful ideas

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.640
or the use of words that some see as hateful.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.300
Those are indeed generally protected by the
First Amendment.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.080
But why?

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.180
The justices generally agree that racist ideas,
for instance, are wrong and dangerous.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.980
Why would the justices say hate speech is
constitutionally protected?

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:49.520
Because they don't trust government officials
to decide which ideas are wrong and dangerous.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.860
They worry that if government officials had
the power to ban evil ideas

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.560
that power would quickly stretch to punishing
a wide range of debate and dissent.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.720
And they see the First Amendment as requiring
that distrust.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.200
In the words of Justice Black, echoed by the
Supreme Court in 1972:

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.160
"The freedoms guaranteed by the First Amendment
must be accorded to the ideas we hate

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.180
or sooner or later they will be denied to
the ideas we cherish."

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.160
So to sum up, there is no hate speech exception
to the First Amendment,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.720
threats of violence and incitement to violence
are not protected

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.220
but that has nothing to do with their hateful
content,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.760
and hate crime laws can punish violence or
vandalism

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.980
based on the offender targeting particular
groups

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.360
but that doesn't allow punishment of supposed
hate speech.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.200
I'm Eugene Volokh and I approve this message.

